# oRPC Server Routers

## Intent
Keep every server-side oRPC router consistent so new domains drop into `packages/server/src/routers` without guessing about folder shapes, procedure wiring, or authentication metadata.

## Scope
- `packages/server/src/routers/**`
- `packages/server/src/context.ts` and `@/server/procedure/*`
- `packages/server/src/routers/_app.ts`

## Procedure Types

The server exposes four procedure types with different authentication and authorization requirements:

### `publicProcedure`
- No authentication required
- Use for endpoints accessible by anyone (e.g., health checks, public data)
- No `.meta()` needed

### `authenticatedProcedure`
- Requires user to be logged in
- Adds `session` to context with non-null user and session data
- No `.meta()` needed (authentication is automatic)
- Use for any endpoint that needs a logged-in user but no role-based or organization-based access

### `adminProcedure`
- Requires user to be logged in AND have a specific role
- **MUST** include `.meta({ roles: [...] })` or the middleware throws an error
- Use for admin-only endpoints (e.g., user management, system settings)
- Available roles are defined in `@repo/database` as `UserRole` (e.g., `"admin"`, `"user"`)

### `organizationProcedure`
- Requires user to be logged in, have an active organization, AND have specific permissions
- **MUST** include `.meta({ permission: { resource, actions } })` or the middleware throws an error
- Adds `activeOrganization` to context with full organization data
- Use for organization-scoped endpoints (e.g., project management, team settings)
- Resources and actions are defined in `@repo/database/organization/permissions`

## Checklist
1. Create one folder per domain under `packages/server/src/routers/<domain>` with `queries/`, `mutations/`, `validators.ts`, and `router.ts`.
2. Inside `queries/` and `mutations/`, add an `index.ts` that re-exports every route file so `router.ts` only imports from the folder root.
3. In each route file export exactly two symbols: `<routeName>Base` (the procedure) and `<routeName>Handler` (the resolver created via `.handler`).
4. Build procedures with `publicProcedure`, `authenticatedProcedure`, `adminProcedure`, or `organizationProcedure`, chaining `.input(schema)` only when the route accepts data.
5. Never wrap handlers in `try/catch`; rely on the shared error registry configured in `base` and throw domain errors or use `base.errors.*`.
6. Compose routes inside `router.ts` by importing the bases and handlers, then calling `base.router({ ... })` with per-route `.route({ method })`, optional `.meta`, and `.handler(call(...))`.
7. Lift every domain router into `packages/server/src/routers/_app.ts` and re-export `appRouter` (and its type) from `@repo/server` for client consumption.

## Folder Layout
Each domain folder must follow the same structure:

```
routers/
  auth/
    mutations/
      index.ts
      resend-invitation.ts
    queries/
      index.ts
      list-users.ts
    router.ts
    validators.ts
```

Route files stay flat (one file → one procedure) so you can reason about inputs, auth, and handlers at a glance.

## Procedure Files
- Define the base with the most specific procedure helper you need
- Define the handler by calling `.handler` on the base
- Access context (database, session, headers, activeOrganization) through the destructured arguments

## Router File Requirements
- Import `base` from `@/server/context` and every `{ <name>Base, <name>Handler }` from the local `queries`/`mutations` indices.
- Instantiate a router via `export const userRouter = base.router({ ... })`.
- Each property name becomes the RPC path segment (`user.listUsers`, `user.updateProfile`, etc.).
- Chain `.route({ method: "GET" | "POST" | "PATCH" | "DELETE" })` before `.meta`/`.handler` to keep HTTP verbs explicit.
- Finish with `.handler(async ({ input, context }) => call(<name>Handler, input, { context }))` so the reusable handler stays testable.

## Examples

### Public Endpoint (no auth)
```ts
import { publicProcedure } from "../../../procedure/public.procedure";

export const healthCheckBase = publicProcedure;

export const healthCheckHandler = healthCheckBase.handler(async () => {
  return { status: "ok" };
});
```

### Authenticated Endpoint (logged-in user required)
```ts
import { authenticatedProcedure } from "../../../procedure/authenticated.procedure";
import { getProfileSchema } from "../validators";

export const getProfileBase = authenticatedProcedure.input(getProfileSchema);

export const getProfileHandler = getProfileBase.handler(async ({ context }) => {
  const { session } = context;
  return { user: session.user };
});
```

### Admin Endpoint (role check required)
```ts
import { adminProcedure } from "../../../procedure/admin.procedure";
import { banUsersSchema } from "../validators";

export const banUsersBase = adminProcedure
  .meta({ roles: ["admin"] })
  .input(banUsersSchema);

export const banUsersHandler = banUsersBase.handler(async ({ input, context }) => {
  const { userRole } = context;
  // userRole is guaranteed to be one of the allowed roles
  return { banned: input.userIds };
});
```

### Organization Endpoint (permission check required)
```ts
import { organizationProcedure } from "../../../procedure/organization.procedure";
import { createProjectSchema } from "../validators";

export const createProjectBase = organizationProcedure
  .meta({ permission: { resource: "project", actions: ["create"] } })
  .input(createProjectSchema);

export const createProjectHandler = createProjectBase.handler(async ({ input, context }) => {
  const { activeOrganization, session } = context;
  // activeOrganization is guaranteed to exist and user has "create" permission on "project"
  return { projectId: "...", organizationId: activeOrganization.id };
});
```

## Validators
- Keep every schema inside `<domain>/validators.ts`.
- Export schemas with descriptive names (`listUsersSchema`, `updateMultipleUsersStatusSchema`).
- Reuse them across queries/mutations to guarantee both server validation and inferred client types stay synchronized.

## Aggregation
- `packages/server/src/routers/_app.ts` must merge every domain router into `appRouter` via `base.router({ auth: authRouter, ... })`.
- Re-export `appRouter` (and `AppRouter` type) from `@repo/server` so clients can derive inputs/outputs with `InferRouter*`.
- Any new domain router is added both to `_app.ts` and to whatever barrel file `@repo/server` exposes.

## Good Patterns
- Pair base + handler exports for every procedure to keep validation and resolver logic obvious.
- Keep router files declarative (only `.route/.meta/.handler` chains) and push implementation details into handlers.
- Use procedure helpers that encode auth requirements instead of sprinkling custom checks.
- Share zod schemas between server handlers and any other server utilities (emails, background jobs) to stay type-safe.

## Anti-Patterns
- ❌ Using `adminProcedure` without `.meta({ roles: [...] })` - middleware will throw an error.
- ❌ Using `organizationProcedure` without `.meta({ permission: { resource, actions } })` - middleware will throw an error.
- ❌ Skipping `index.ts` inside `queries/` or `mutations/`, forcing deep relative imports in `router.ts`.
- ❌ Exporting anything other than `<routeName>Base` and `<routeName>Handler` from a route file.
- ❌ Wrapping handlers in `try/catch`—the oRPC stack already maps thrown errors to HTTP responses via `base.errors`.
